+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct visits on tablets that can be offline for 48 hours, and two workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. Their edits reach the server days apart, each made from the same starting state and neither aware of the other. The record store has to say what the visit is once both have arrived.
+
+The previous spreadsheet process answered by overwriting, and lost corrections without showing which. Any policy here keeps every original submission, so nothing is lost outright. What is at stake is subtler: which value reaches the science partner's Friday CSV, who decided it, and how much coordinator time that costs. A wrong answer in one direction exports a visit that no worker wrote. A wrong answer in the other holds correct visits out of the export while they wait in a queue.
+
+The answer also sets the size of two other nodes. Tablet-sync stores and sends whatever the policy needs to detect concurrency. Review-export builds its coordinator screen and export eligibility around whatever the policy leaves unresolved.
+
+How often two workers edit the same visit concurrently has not been measured. The team produces about 6,000 visits a season and has two coordinators.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Both options keep every original submission and work with tablets offline, so neither of those selects between them.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **No exported visit that nobody wrote** (major). A visit's fields are not independent: a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation. The non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent* means the server has no basis for knowing which fields belong together. Derives from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Coordinator review workload and export readiness** (major). Two coordinators clear all review work, and the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* is only useful if the queue can be cleared in time. Derives from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Standing maintenance** (moderate). Rules that must change when the visit's fields change need someone to own them. Derives from the same tenet.
+- **Explaining an exported value** (moderate). How much a coordinator must read to say where a value in the CSV came from. Derives from the inspectability tenet.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size** (minor). What each tablet holds per visit and sends per edit. Derives from the tenet *let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*. At 6,000 small structured visits a season, neither option strains a tablet.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every edit is a complete visit that names the revision it started from. When two revisions were made from the same starting state, the store combines nothing: both stand as competing revisions and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator submits a resolution. The coordinator's resolution is itself a complete visit, which may equal one side or combine both.
+
+Tablet-sync keeps one base revision ID per visit and sends complete visits. Review-export needs a screen that compares complete revisions side by side, and takes exported values only from visits that are not unresolved.
+
+**Demonstration.** One worker corrects an observer's name; another, offline, adds a missing species to the same visit. Both arrive. The visit is unresolved and absent from the CSV until a coordinator writes the visit that contains both corrections.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No exported visit that nobody wrote: **strong**. Every revision, including a resolution, is a complete visit written by one named person.
+- Coordinator review workload and export readiness: **weak**. Every concurrently edited visit reaches a coordinator, including the ones whose edits touch unrelated fields, and each is held out of the export until resolved.
+- Standing maintenance: **strong**. The policy looks only at which revision an edit started from. It does not change when fields are added.
+- Explaining an exported value: **strong**. The value comes from one revision; its history is a chain of whole revisions and named resolutions.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **strong**. One ID per visit; each submission is one complete visit.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator time for the guarantee that a person wrote every visit that is exported.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every edit supplies its starting state and the changes made to it. The server compares the two sets of changes. Where they touch different fields, it combines them into one current visit automatically. Where they touch the same field, that field goes to a coordinator. Combination follows maintained rules per kind of field: scalars such as observation date, the observer list, and species entries keyed by species.
+
+Tablet-sync keeps a base snapshot per visit and sends structured changes. Review-export needs to show, for each field of a visit, which submission supplied it, and a review view limited to the overlapping fields.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same two edits arrive. The server produces a visit with the corrected name and the added species. No coordinator is involved and the visit is in Friday's CSV.
+
+A second case: one worker changes a species identification, the other revises that entry's cover estimate. Whether these overlap depends on how the species-entry rule is written. If it treats them as separate fields, the server exports the new identification with a cover estimate made for the old one.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No exported visit that nobody wrote: **weak**. An automatically combined visit was written by no one, and it is correct only where the rules happen to match the scientific relationships between fields.
+- Coordinator review workload and export readiness: **strong**. Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator; visits with unrelated edits are ready for export without review.
+- Standing maintenance: **weak**. Three kinds of rule to write, and each must be revisited when a field is added or its meaning changes.
+- Explaining an exported value: **partial**. Every value can be traced, but only through field-level provenance, and "the server combined these" is part of the explanation.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. A base snapshot per visit and structured changes; more to hold and send, though small at this scale.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a class of unnoticed scientific errors, and standing rule maintenance, for a short review queue and more visits ready each Friday.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+The two major criteria point in opposite directions, and the choice turns on which failure is visible. A long review queue is counted, listed, and seen by the people who can act on it before Friday. A merge that joined two related edits looks like any other visit in the CSV and is found, if at all, by the science partner. The team prefers the larger queue to rules that can combine scientifically related edits without anyone noticing. This follows the inspectability tenet and accepts a cost against the tenet about what two coordinators can operate; the moderate criteria both favour the same side.
+
+**What it forecloses.** No visit is ready for export while two people's edits to it are outstanding, however unrelated those edits are. The store has no partial submissions and no field-level provenance.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- Tablet-sync keeps the base revision ID for each visit it holds, sends complete visits, and shows a worker when their visit is unresolved and what it competes with.
+- Review-export provides a comparison of complete revisions in which a coordinator can write the resolved visit, and holds unresolved visits out of the CSV. It must make the queue visible early enough to be cleared before Friday, because that queue is where this decision's cost lands.
+
+**What would reopen it.** Field-aware merging remains a credible design for this problem, and three observations would justify returning to it:
+
+- Unresolved visits are regularly still in the queue at Friday's export.
+- Coordinators' resolutions are mostly mechanical: they combine two edits to unrelated fields without changing either.
+- The science partner or the team can state which fields form one observation, so that merge rules would rest on stated relationships and not on field names.
+
+The first two can be read from the store's revision history. Returning would not require new data from the tablets: each revision is a complete visit that names its base, so the base and both sides of any conflict are already stored. The cost would fall on the merge rules and on field-level provenance in review-export.
