-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions: concurrent edits stand as competing complete revisions until a coordinator resolves them | Field-aware three-way merge: combine disjoint field changes automatically, review only overlaps | Merge rules can combine scientifically related edits, such as a species identification and its cover estimate, without anyone noticing, and the rules need ongoing maintenance for scalars, observer lists, and species entries. A larger review queue is the accepted price: every edit to a concurrently edited visit reaches a coordinator, including edits to unrelated fields. The queue is visible work; a wrong merge is invisible. Full analysis, downstream obligations, and what would reopen the choice: [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Current revision of an unresolved visit | None | First-accepted competing revision stays current; last agreed revision stays current | Either alternative lets a value that a worker has contested flow to export, and the first ranks workers by arrival order. |
+| Identical competing payloads | Still competing | Settle automatically when all competing payloads are equal | The state rule depends only on bases, so a visit's state can be explained without comparing content. |
+| Resolution payload | Coordinator supplies a complete visit | Coordinator must pick one competing revision unchanged | Picking one discards the other worker's unrelated correction, which is the usual shape of a conflict. |
+| Resolution that missed a newly arrived revision | Rejected with the present competing revisions | Accept it and leave the visit unresolved against the newcomer | A coordinator's decision should cover everything submitted; coordinators work online and can review again at once. |
+| Edit with a stale base | Accepted as a competing revision | Reject and require the tablet to refetch and redo the edit | The edit is a worker's submitted observation; rejecting it pushes reconciliation onto a field worker who may be offline again. |
+| Revision IDs | Server-assigned at acceptance | Client-generated | The ID then exists only for accepted revisions, and the idempotency key already covers retries. Costs tablet-sync in-order submission of queued edits to one visit. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
